{
  "name": "What to Do When Your Foreclosed Property is Missing Flooring",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "It's pretty common for foreclosed properties to have missing flooring, and figuring out what to do next can feel like a big hurdle. First things first, you'll want to get a good look at the subfloor. Is it damaged? Are there any signs of moisture or mold? These aren't just aesthetic issues; sometimes, missing floor coverings can even be considered a health and safety concern in foreclosed property transactions, and may need to be addressed before closing. Once you've got a handle on the subfloor's condition, the next step is getting a detailed, line-item quote. Make sure it breaks down everything: materials, labor, demolition, disposal, and any prep work needed. Don't assume anything is included! You’ll also need to consider material lead times and how that impacts your overall project timeline. When you're ready to schedule, factor in demolition, installation, and thorough cleanup. For situations involving moisture, mold, or uneven subfloors, or if you suspect hidden damage, that’s when a professional really shines. They can spot potential issues you might miss and ensure the job is done right and safely. If you’re unsure, give HH Remodeling LLC a call for an inspection, we're happy to help you understand the scope.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
